Tuple
----
  - It is used to store group of elements including duplicates
  - Tuples never allowed modifications
  - Tuples are immutable object
  - Tuples are declared by using ()
Example
  t1 = (5,7,9)
- We have both forward and backward index to access elements in tuple
- Forward index always starts from 0
- Backward index always starts from -1 from last element onwards
- Once you declare tuple then we can not add or delete or modify elements

How to Declare a tuple with single element
------------------------------------------
  t1 = (7)
  print(type(t1))      # int
  
  t2 = (6,)
  print(type(t2))      # tuple

How to modifie the tuple data
-----------------------------
  t1 = ('CSE', 'IT', 'ECE')
  
  Now add 'EEE' course to your tuple.
  
  Directly we can't add any element to Tuple
  Because tuples are immutable objects so we can't do modifications
  If you want to add element then convert tuple to list then add element
  t1 = ('CSE','IT','ECE')
  
  print("Your Courses are : ", t1)
  
  t1 = list(t1)
  t1.append('EEE')
  t1 = tuple(t1)
  print("Your Courses are : ", t1)


-------------------------------
TUPLE OPERATIONS
-------------------------------
t = (10, 20, 30, 20, 40)
Access element by index
  print(t[0])                    # 10
Slicing
  print(t[1:4])                  # (20, 30, 20)
Concatenation
  print(t + (50, 60))            # (10, 20, 30, 20, 40, 50, 60)
Repetition
  print(t * 2)                   # (10, 20, 30, 20, 40, 10, 20, 30, 20, 40)
Membership
  print(30 in t)                 # True
Iteration
  for i in t:
    print(i)

-------------------------------
 TUPLE BUILT-IN FUNCTIONS
 -------------------------------
t = (10, 20, 30, 20, 40)
len() – Number of elements
  print(len(t))                  # 5

type() – Data type
  print(type(t))                 # <class 'tuple'>

max() – Maximum element
  print(max(t))                  # 40

min() – Minimum element
  print(min(t))                  # 10

sum() – Sum of elements
  print(sum(t))                  # 120

sorted() – Returns sorted list
  print(sorted(t))               # [10, 20, 20, 30, 40]

tuple() – Convert to tuple
  print(tuple([1, 2, 3]))        # (1, 2, 3)

any() – True if any element is True
  print(any(t))                  # True

all() – True if all elements are True
  print(all(t))                  # True

Methods
--------
 -We have only 2 methods available in tuples, they are:

  1. count()
  It is used to count no. of occurrences of a given element
  2. index()
  It is used to find index number of a given element
  Example
  t1 = (5, 9, 7, 4, 5)
  
  print('9 occured', t1.count(9), 'times') #9 occured 1 times
  print('9 Available in index number', t1.index(9)) #9 Available in index number 1
  print('8 occured', t1.count(8), 'times') #8 occured 0 times

Difference Between List and Tuple
-----------------------------------
List
----
-List declared by using []
-List is a Mutable Object
-That means we can do modifications
-Methods in List
  append()
  insert()
  remove()
  pop(index)

Tuple
-----
-Tuples are declared by using ()
-Tuple are Immutable objects
-That means we can not modify tuple elements
-Methods in Tuple
  count()
  index()



